                    </p>

                    <hr {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleRule)} />

                    <h2 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleSectionHeading)}>Incremental learning</h2>

                    <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleParagraph)}>
                        If the model can't learn new diseases after it's
                        deployed, it will go out of date. So I built incremental
                        learning with four strategies, each implementing the
                        same generic Rust trait:
                    </p>

                    <pre {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.monospace, blogStyles.codeBlock)}><code {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.monospace, blogStyles.inlineCode, blogStyles.codeInsideBlock)}>pub trait IncrementalLearner&lt;B: Backend&gt; &lbrace;
    fn learn_incremental(
        &amp;mut self,
        new_data: &amp;[DataPoint],
        config: &amp;IncrementalConfig,
    ) -&gt; IncrementalResult;
&rbrace;</code></pre>

                    <div {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.monospace, blogStyles.methodsGrid)}>
                        <div {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodCard)}>
                            <h4 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleMinorHeading)}>Fine-tuning</h4>
                            <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodDescription)}>
                                Retrain on the new data. It's simple, but the model can
                                forget classes it learned before (catastrophic
                                forgetting).
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodCard)}>
                            <h4 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleMinorHeading)}>LwF (Learning without Forgetting)</h4>
                            <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodDescription)}>
                                Distills knowledge from the old model, so it keeps what
                                it learned without storing the old training
                                data.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodCard)}>
                            <h4 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleMinorHeading)}>EWC (Elastic Weight Consolidation)</h4>
                            <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodDescription)}>
                                Uses the Fisher information matrix to penalize changes
                                to important weights. It needs little memory and
                                has a solid theoretical basis.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                        <div {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodCard)}>
                            <h4 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleMinorHeading)}>Rehearsal</h4>
                            <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.methodDescription)}>
                                Replays stored samples from earlier tasks, picked by
                                herding, at random, or by distance.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleParagraph)}>
                        With this, a farmer could photograph a new disease
                        variant, have an expert label a small batch, and the
                        model would update on the device itself, without going
                        through the cloud or being redeployed.
                    </p>

                    <hr {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleRule)} />

                    <h2 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleSectionHeading)}>What I learned</h2>

                    <ul {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleList)}>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            Burn is ready for production inference. The API is
                            clean, switching backends works, and the community
                            responds quickly. Training is still less comfortable
                            than in the established frameworks.
                        </li>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            For the browser, ONNX was the practical route.
                            Exporting to ONNX and letting ONNX Runtime Web run
                            the model worked, and fighting to compile Burn to
                            WASM didn't.
                        </li>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            Dedicated edge hardware is often overkill. The
                            &euro;350 Jetson was slower than the phone, and for a
                            model this size, ordinary consumer devices are fast
                            enough for most inference.
                        </li>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            Preprocessing has to match exactly. A small difference
                            in resize interpolation between training and
                            inference wrecks accuracy without any error, so every
                            target needs the exact same preprocessing.
                        </li>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            Rust's compile times are a real cost. Expect release
                            builds of 5+ minutes. Incremental debug builds are
                            fast, but CI and releases are slow.
                        </li>
                        <li {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleListItem)}>
                            Tauri v2 works well for ML apps. A Rust backend with a
                            web frontend gives you native GPU access and modern UI
                            tools, and the command/event system connects the two
                            cleanly.
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <hr {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleRule)} />

                    <h2 {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleSectionHeading)}>Conclusion</h2>

                    <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleParagraph)}>
                        The project started with the question "can we run ML on
                        a farm?" and turned into a long look at Rust's ML
                        ecosystem. The answer is yes, with caveats.
                    </p>

                    <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleParagraph)}>
                        With semi-supervised learning, 20% labeled data gave
                        results comparable to training on 60% labeled data.
                        Burn compiled the same model for desktop GPU and CPU,
                        and through ONNX it also runs in the browser. Tauri v2
                        turned all of that into a real application with a
                        proper GUI.
                    </p>

                    <p {...stylex.attrs(blogStyles.articleParagraph)}>
                        Rust's ML ecosystem is younger than Python's and still
                        has rough edges. For getting a trained model onto
                        limited devices that have to work offline, though, it
                        did what I needed. Data loading, augmentation, training,
                        and inference are all in one language, and that made
                        the project a lot simpler to work on.
                    </p>

